Pick what fits you (I can give exact step-by-step if you tell me which calendar app you use—Google Calendar, Apple Calendar, Outlook, etc.).

1) Decide a “single source of truth”
- Create one dedicated calendar (or calendar folder) named “Family & Friends — Dates” instead of scattering entries across work/personal calendars.
- Pros: easy to filter, share, and back up.

2) Capture contacts first
- Add each person as a Contact (name, phone, email, birthday). For babies add parent names, baby’s birthdate, gender, registry link, and any gift notes.
- Why: many calendar apps can auto-sync birthdays from Contacts → Calendar.

3) Add the date events (recommended fields)
For each person/event create a calendar event using this template:
- Title: “David & [Partner] — Baby Jasper born (Jasper’s bday: YYYY-MM-DD)” or “Ava — Birthday”
- Calendar: Family & Friends — Dates
- Description: address, gift ideas, registry link, best times to call, notes
- Location: postal address (if you send a card)
- Recurrence: yearly for birthdays/anniversaries; single or custom for milestones
- Reminders: set at appropriate intervals (see rules below)

4) Reminder rules (examples that actually work)
- Birthdays / Anniversaries (yearly):
  - 4 weeks before → “order/send gift / schedule card”
  - 1 week before → “buy gift / wrap”
  - Day of → morning reminder to call/message
- New baby (first-year milestones):
  - Birth date (one-time + yearly): immediate thank-you / send card
  - 1 month, 3 months, 6 months, 9 months → gentle reminder to send a message / small gift
  - 1 year → bigger reminder (party / milestone gift)
- Use fewer alerts if you get overwhelmed (e.g., 1 month + day-of) and let recurring tasks handle the rest.

5) Useful automations & shortcuts
- Contacts → Birthdays: enable your calendar’s “Birthdays” layer so new contact birthdays show automatically.
- Spreadsheet + Zapier/IFTTT: keep a Google Sheet of dates and use a Zap to auto-create calendar events when you add a row.
- Save gift ideas or links inside the event description (clickable registry links).
- Use a “card ordered” checkbox in a task app (Todoist, Any.do) linked to the event for execution tracking.

6) Apps and tools (pick one or two)
- Calendars: Google Calendar (best for cross-device + Gmail/Contacts), Apple Calendar (best if you’re in Apple ecosystem), Outlook (best for Microsoft users).
- Task/Reminder apps: Todoist, Any.do, Google Tasks — integrate with your calendar for actionable reminders.
- Shared/family planning: Cozi (great family calendar + shared tasks/meal plans).
- Birthday/gift trackers: Trello or a simple Google Sheet for tracking purchased/sent gifts and budgets.
- Privacy note: store registry links or personal details in a private note field or password manager entry rather than public calendar descriptions if the calendar is shared.

7) Physical backup & rituals
- Keep a small physical planner or reading journal where you note the next month’s events during a monthly review — helps you feel in control.
- Monthly review habit: spend 10–15 minutes on the 1st of each month to scan upcoming events and prepare.

